BT 1 0 0 1 24 200 Tm /F4 10 Tf 15 TL 1.626667 Tw (Wine regions are classified in two fundamentally different ways: by what the land gives them \227) Tj T* 0 Tw .931429 Tw (geology, climate, topography \227 and by what they have made of themselves \227 narrative, ambition,) Tj T* 0 Tw .359231 Tw (historical depth, cultural character. This study asks whether these two modes of classification agree.) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.708333 Tw (Across 59 wine regions spanning 16 countries, we construct independent clustering solutions for) Tj T* 0 Tw .462308 Tw (terroir \(Layer 2: six factual geographic and climatic features, TF-IDF vectorised, k=7\) and cultural) Tj T* 0 Tw 3.588462 Tw (identity \(Layer 1: six expert-scored identity dimensions \227 D1 through D6 \227 derived from) Tj T* 0 Tw .0375 Tw (anthropological descriptions, k=6\). Testing their agreement using the Adjusted Rand Index \(ARI\), the) Tj T* 0 Tw .598571 Tw (result is unambiguous: ARI = 0.023, chi-squared p = 0.131. The two classifications are statistically) Tj T* 0 Tw -0.104286 Tw (independent. A region\222s physical terroir tells us almost nothing about its cultural identity cluster. This) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.035 Tw (finding places wine region analysis within a broader family of dual-classification studies spanning) Tj T* 0 Tw 2.045 Tw (cross-cultural psychology, historical linguistics, biogeography, and neuroscience, where physical) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.023846 Tw (substrate and emergent identity repeatedly diverge. For the wine world, the implication is precise:) Tj T* 0 Tw 1.877692 Tw (terroir shapes what grapes can become; history, ambition, and culture determine what a region) Tj T* 0 Tw (chooses to be.) Tj T* ET
